    title: 'Next Time: Chapter 23 — The Proposal',
    preview: 'On a beautiful midsummer evening, Jane walks in the orchard while Rochester smokes his cigar nearby. The air is heavy with scent of flowers, the atmosphere charged with everything unspoken between them. Rochester calls Jane to him, and in one of literature\'s most famous scenes, he finally confesses his love—but not before testing Jane by pretending he\'ll send her away to work for another family in Ireland while he marries Blanche Ingram. Jane\'s passionate outburst reveals her feelings: "Do you think I am an automaton?—a machine without feelings? Do you think, because I am poor, obscure, plain, and little, I am soulless and heartless? You think wrong!—I have as much soul as you,—and full as much heart!" Rochester drops the pretense and proposes. Jane accepts, but nature itself seems to protest: a storm breaks, lightning strikes the great chestnut tree under which they stood, splitting it in two. The omen is ominous, foreshadowing disaster to come. Jane has said yes to the man she loves, not knowing he\'s already married to the mad woman locked in the attic.',
